export PURPOSE:
    A dashboard for monitoring an orchestrated build across agents and
    harnesses. This package supplies an initial specification to tether
    into another application's specification. It is a general lattice to
    build from and adapt to that application, not a drop-in blueprint
    to reproduce unchanged.

export ADAPTATION_GUIDANCE:
    principle:
        Every consuming workflow is different: its orchestration, agents,
        harnesses, build execution, telemetry sources, usage limits, and
        interface conventions will not match this package exactly. Build
        the parts that make sense for that workflow, take the rest as
        guidance for building the equivalent thing there, and adapt the
        form while preserving the intent behind each requirement.
    howToApply:
        - Start from the consuming application's actual workflow and data, not from this package's assumed shape.
        - Build capabilities, sources, and sections that fit; omit or defer those that do not apply rather than forcing them in.
        - Adapt presentation, reporting transport and cadence, breakdowns, limits, and operational defaults to local conventions and available telemetry.
        - Where a requirement is adapted or omitted, record the decision and its reason in the consuming specification, and preserve its intent in whatever form replaces it.
    firmInvariants:
        - Never present fabricated, estimated, or unavailable values as observed telemetry; keep observed, estimated, unknown, and stale values distinct.
        - Do not count the same work or usage twice when combining sources, and preserve task, agent, harness, and run identity.
        - Keep the dashboard observational unless the consuming application separately decides otherwise.
    flexibleByDesign:
        - Which harnesses, telemetry sources, and local or remote builds are monitored.
        - Which sections, tables, charts, breakdowns, and limits are shown, and how they are laid out.
        - Reporting format and transport, refresh and heartbeat cadence, freshness thresholds, and cost and projection methods.

export DROP_IN_CONTRACT:
    purpose:
        This package specifies a dashboard that drops into an existing
        agentic codebase. This contract states what the dashboard may
        assume from its host, what it must discover or adapt to, and
        what it guarantees in return.
    minimumHostAssumption:
        The dashboard may assume only that the host can identify its
        work stably: runs, tasks, agents, and attempts have identities,
        and the host can say when each starts, progresses, and ends.
        It may not assume any particular harness, build service, usage
        or quota source, reporting format, or user-interface stack.
        Everything beyond identity and lifecycle is discovered from
        the host as it actually is, and stays unknown where the host
        cannot supply it.
    integrationBoundary:
        The core dashboard is read-only: it works by observing the
        host's existing identity, lifecycle, and telemetry without
        requiring changes to the host. Adding hooks, reporters, or
        other instrumentation is optional enrichment that only fills
        in metrics which would otherwise remain unknown; the absence
        of such instrumentation never makes an installation failed
        or incomplete.
    workModelMapping:
        Host work is mapped once into the canonical model of runs,
        tasks, agents, and attempts. Nesting, parallel branches, and
        retries remain visible rather than being flattened into their
        parent. Work that cannot be mapped appears as its own row with
        unknown fields; it is never hidden and never forced into a
        shape it does not have.
    coreGuarantee:
        From identity and lifecycle alone, a viewer can see what work
        is running, how it is nested and grouped, its status, and its
        running time. Progress, estimates, software builds, usage,
        quotas, projections, and costs are opportunistic: each appears
        where a discovered source supports it and stays unknown where
        none does.
    nonInterference:
        Installing the dashboard must never put host work at risk. It
        is read-only and failure-isolated: its own failure, slowness,
        polling, or a broken source adapter never delays, alters, or
        stops agent work, software builds, or task execution. A failing
        source degrades to stale or unknown on the dashboard only.

export INTENDED_USERS:
    People watching an orchestrated build who need visibility into the
    work currently running and the usage limits that may affect it.

export CAPABILITIES:
    - Show currently running tasks, broken down across agents and harnesses.
    - Show running time for tasks.
    - Show estimated task progress and time to completion where possible.
    - Show usage limits and projections for when those limits may be hit.
    - Provide comprehensive usage totals, trends, breakdowns, quotas, and available costs.
    - Show local and remote software builds used by the consuming pipeline.
    - Show agent-reported percent complete for running tasks.
    - Rebuild usage history for the whole orchestration from harness-local records.
    - Estimate costs from published token rates, with their source and date.
    - Find and close dead or stuck work automatically.

export SCOPE:
    - This specification is guidance to adapt to each consuming workflow, not a uniform checklist every application must reproduce in full.
    - The dashboard monitors builds only.
    - Pausing tasks, cancelling runs, and reassigning work are outside scope.
    - This package supplies a specification and an implementation skill.
    - Dashboard implementation happens in the consuming application when requested.

export INITIAL_SUCCESS_CRITERIA:
    - A viewer can identify running tasks and their agents and harnesses.
    - A viewer can see task running time and available progress and ETA estimates.
    - A viewer can see usage limits and available projections for reaching them.
    - Estimates and unknown values follow the estimate policy.
    - The specification can be tethered into the consuming application's spec.

export LIMITS_QUESTION:
    - Which usage limits and reset periods must be represented?

export REFRESH_QUESTION:
    - How frequently should the UI refresh independently of agent reporting?

export INTEGRATION_QUESTION:
    - How will the consuming application's specification reference this package?
